1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,530
So this is the moment you have been waiting for.

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:06,200
It's time to create our first project.

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:11,540
To make a new project, all you need to do is click the big create button at the top of the page, and

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:16,820
this creates a brand new project for you and takes you to the Scratch Project editor, where all the

5
00:00:16,820 --> 00:00:18,560
awesome stuff happens.

6
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:23,900
Now that we're in the place where all the action happens, let's take a tour so you can get a quick

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,240
understanding of how it all works.

8
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:30,560
So this whole area that we're in at the moment is called The editor.

9
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:33,230
And this is where you can design and code your project.

10
00:00:33,230 --> 00:00:37,340
It's basically where you will create anything you can imagine.

11
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:46,220
The scratch editor has four main parts the stage, the sprites, the code blocks, and the scripts.

12
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:49,040
Let me explain what each part does.

13
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,860
All right, let's first talk about the stage area.

14
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:55,100
You'll find it on the far right of your screen.

15
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,830
The stage is where all the fun stuff happens.

16
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,900
Just like a stage in a theater.

17
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:03,890
This is where you see the action as you create your project.

18
00:01:03,890 --> 00:01:06,350
Everything you make will appear right here.

19
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:10,880
It's the special space where you can see and build your amazing project.

20
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:16,580
Now, looking down from the stage, you will see this area here where you can choose the background

21
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:17,510
of the stage.

22
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:23,150
Currently we just have this boring plain background selected, but if you click on this little button

23
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:28,670
on the bottom right, you can choose a new backdrop, plus add sounds and music to the stage.

24
00:01:28,670 --> 00:01:31,370
But we'll explore all that in a future lesson.

25
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:36,380
A scratch sprite is the character that your scratch code controls.

26
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:41,570
For example, you may write some code to make your sprite move around or talk.

27
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:46,580
These sprites can be all sorts of things, ranging from people to animals to objects.

28
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:53,360
And just like with backdrops for the stage, you can choose from loads of pre-made sprites or even draw

29
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:55,370
your very own unique wines.

30
00:01:55,370 --> 00:02:00,740
Plus, you can even upload sprites from your computer, which we'll be doing in future lessons.

31
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:02,880
Next are the code blocks.

32
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,990
These are the instructions that you can use to make your sprites do things.

33
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:13,620
It's just like real world programming, but instead of typing out complex lines of text, you use these

34
00:02:13,620 --> 00:02:14,220
blocks.

35
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:17,520
But essentially you're doing the same thing you're coding.

36
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:25,650
There are different categories of blocks such as motion looks, sound events, control, and many more.

37
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:32,620
Each category represents a different aspect of programming, helping you to build a complete and interactive

38
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:33,280
project.

39
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:39,670
To use these code blocks, simply drag and drop them from the block palette into the script area, and

40
00:02:39,670 --> 00:02:43,960
as you put the blocks together, you will start to see your project come to life.

41
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:49,630
And speaking of the scripts area, this is the last major area we must discuss.

42
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:55,150
This is where we put blocks together in a special order that tells your sprites what to do.

43
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:59,050
You can connect code blocks together by snapping them like puzzle pieces.

44
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:04,660
Just as a quick example, I will bring this move ten steps block onto my window here.

45
00:03:04,660 --> 00:03:09,160
As you can see, if I click it, my cat sprite moves forward on the screen.

46
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:17,140
And lastly, if you look to the very top, you will see that there are these different tabs for costumes

47
00:03:17,140 --> 00:03:18,370
and sounds.

48
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:24,040
Now these are also very important spaces that will help us create stunning projects, but we will get

49
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:25,630
into that later on.

50
00:03:25,630 --> 00:03:30,820
But for now, that's a quick overview of the scratch interface and how it all works and carries you

51
00:03:30,820 --> 00:03:36,190
to play around and check out things for yourself, because that's where the learning happens in the

52
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:36,850
doing.

53
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:42,910
I look forward to seeing you in the next lesson, where we will start to see what all this code does.

